            <ArticleTitle>Associations of chronic hepatitis C with metabolic and cardiac outcomes.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>647-52</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/apt.12234</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Chronic hepatitis C virus (CH-C) infection is associated with metabolic conditions such as insulin resistance and type 2 diabetes (DM) and may increase the risk of cardiovascular diseases.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To assess the association of CH-C with risk factors for cardiovascular diseases using US population data.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The National Health and Nutrition Examination Surveys (NHANES) collected between 1999 and 2010 were used.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Of 19 741 participants considered eligible for the study. Of this cohort, 173 individuals (0.88%) had detectable HCV RNA and were considered to have CH-C. Compared with controls, CH-C patients were predominantly African American (23.5% vs. 10.5%, P &lt; 0.0001), men (66.6% vs. 46.1%, P = 0.0001), more likely to be between 45 and 55 years of age (41.9% vs. 20.4%, P = 0.0001), had higher rate of insulin resistance (44.1% vs. 31.1%, P = 0.0301), hypertension (40.1% vs. 28.9%, P = 0.0201), and history of smoking (76.2% vs. 29.9%, P &lt; 0.0001). In multivariate analysis, in addition to known risk factors for insulin resistance, CH-C was independently associated with the presence of insulin resistance [OR (95% CI) = 2.06 (1.19-3.57)], DM [OR = 2.31 (1.18-4.54)] and hypertension [OR = 2.06 (1.30-3.24)]. Independent predictors of cardiovascular diseases included older age, presence of obesity and smoking. CH-C was independently associated with congestive heart failure subtype of cardiovascular diseases but not ischaemic heart disease and stroke.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Chronic hepatitis C virus infection is independently associated with presence of metabolic conditions (insulin resistance, type 2 diabetes mellitus and hypertension) and congestive heart failure.</AbstractText>
